x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction. A case that includes a C++ standard library header is compiled by clang against clang own library, not this one - so the two objects hold the symbols of two different implementations, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all. The oracle for these cases is behaviour, not names: run.sh hands the same program to both compilers and compares what it prints.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction. A case that includes a C++ standard library header is compiled by clang against clang own library, not this one - so the two objects hold the symbols of two different implementations, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all. The oracle for these cases is behaviour, not names: run.sh hands the same program to both compilers and compares what it prints.
